{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793372057,"nonce":"mdDcHQNmfmCjCwcYtGgNkrlE5aY=","title":"Surface preview protocol: frames, input and lifecycle between a headless app and RayDE","message":"Define the versioned protocol a producer (the shim inside the app) and RayDE speak over stdio, relayed by the session. Messages: hello (framework, version, capabilities), surface (size, scale, format), frame (sequence, damage rectangles, encoding, payload), input (pointer, key, wheel, touch, focus, resize, visibility), pause and resume, log lines for Output, exit. Frames are binary and length-prefixed; the existing process events are base64 in JSON, so measure whether that is enough before adding a binary side channel to the session. Rules: latest frame wins, bounded queues with a sequence acknowledgement for backpressure, the producer renders only while a surface is visible, no unbounded memory on either side. Include a conformance suite any producer must pass against a synthetic app, and a fake producer for RayDE's own checks. Evidence to gather first: bytes per frame, frames per second and added latency through a local process, then through a session over WebSocket.\n\nPart of the framework previews epic: git-bug 9c6f60e27c2cb4be92d534eccc95910e303a5c4e03e1e8779c6d52ca9c40a330","files":null}]}